<section>
  <div class="wrap prose">
    <div class="eyebrow">Who this is for</div>
    <h2 class="mt-0">Change that a handful of quick actions won&rsquo;t solve.</h2>
    <p>Strategy as a Service is built for organizations facing a change that requires sustained, long-term planning where every action taken is deliberately pulling toward a single long-term goal. That, in essence, is our working definition of strategy implementation: a highly pragmatic approach that combines a long-term perspective with the rigor of focused, disciplined execution.</p>
    <p>In practice, the change need usually falls into one of a few categories:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Building or improving a commercial engine</strong> &mdash; sales, growth, and go-to-market performance</li>
      <li><strong>Evolving the value proposition</strong> &mdash; expanding into a new product, service, or market segment</li>
      <li><strong>Changing ways of working (culture)</strong> &mdash; where current habits and structures are holding back innovation or growth</li>
    </ul>
    <p>More broadly, it&rsquo;s for anything the organization considers a critical mission &mdash; something that can&rsquo;t be handled through the typical operational cadence.</p>
    <p>We&rsquo;ve applied this model to companies ranging from $25 million to $2 billion in revenue, with occasional outliers north of $50 billion (typically a division or region within a much larger company). It&rsquo;s scalable because it&rsquo;s built around how industrial and business teams actually operate day to day &mdash; and because those teams, across industries, tend to share remarkably similar blind spots. Strategy as a Service doesn&rsquo;t replace the team; it complements it, adding a layer of perspective that brings more commitment and more ownership to the work already underway.</p>
    <p>Just as important, it&rsquo;s <strong>mission-based and time-bound</strong>. It has a clearly defined goal, a clearly defined outcome, and a clear end date &mdash; a deliberate departure from traditional strategy programs, which tend to be broad and therefore require maintenance across multiple leadership generations. Strategy as a Service is scoped so that a single management generation can see it through, start to finish.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<div class="section-line"></div>

<section>
  <div class="wrap prose">
    <div class="eyebrow">Why strategy needs a different delivery model</div>
    <h2 class="mt-0">Most strategy engagements fail for a predictable reason.</h2>
    <p>They produce an inspirational deck of slides, consume months of the team&rsquo;s energy, and then quietly stall for lack of implementation support. The company is left with a &ldquo;dud&rdquo; &mdash; a failed investment and a team that&rsquo;s now more skeptical of the next initiative than before.</p>

    <div class="stat-callout">
      <div class="fig">95%</div>
      <p class="say">Our starting belief is simple: <strong>95% of the value of any strategy lies in its delivery.</strong></p>
    </div>

    <p>Creating a strategy &mdash; the workshops, the vision statement, the roadmap &mdash; is, frankly, not the hard part. Pulling people out of their day-to-day, giving them room to think, and building alignment generates real energy and is something teams consistently enjoy. The hard part is what happens in month four, and month ten, and month sixteen, when the initial energy fades and the daily pressure of running the business reasserts itself.</p>
    <p>Strategy as a Service exists to solve that specific problem. It borrows its operating model from agile software development: teams work in increments, output is reviewed and measured monthly, and roadblocks are addressed the moment they appear &mdash; not discovered a year later in a post-mortem.</p>
  </div>
</section>

