<section>
  <div class="wrap prose">
    <div class="eyebrow">Why &ldquo;as a service&rdquo;</div>
    <h2 class="mt-0">Not a one-time engagement.</h2>
    <p>It&rsquo;s genuinely difficult for any company &mdash; a $25 million business or a $2 billion one &mdash; to commit to a six-figure strategy fee up front, with the real risk of ending up three months later with an inspirational deck, a team that invested significant energy, and no implementation support to show for it.</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Agile, not waterfall.</strong> Work happens in increments and sprints, output-focused and quality-reviewed monthly.</li>
      <li><strong>Adaptive by design.</strong> When a roadblock emerges, we address it the moment it appears &mdash; not months later.</li>
      <li><strong>Lower upfront commitment.</strong> Payments are spread across the engagement, and clients can cancel at any time.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>We&rsquo;re comfortable offering that flexibility because, once the strategy document is set &mdash; typically around the three-month mark &mdash; the second half of our value delivery begins: showing up every week to keep the Project Management Office focused and moving. Clients who&rsquo;ve experienced that phase rarely walk away, because that&rsquo;s precisely where most strategy initiatives elsewhere quietly die.</p>
    <h2>How long it takes</h2>
    <p>Most engagements run <strong>12 to 18 months</strong>, depending on scope. Twelve months is the minimum we&rsquo;ve found for a genuinely sustainable implementation. The more the initiative requires people to change their actual ways of working &mdash; daily habits, not just priorities &mdash; the closer it moves toward 18 months. As a rule of thumb, 18 months is the realistic minimum for any major organizational step-change.</p>
  </div>
</section>

