<section>
  <div class="wrap prose">
    <div class="eyebrow">What actually happens</div>
    <h2 class="mt-0">Phase 1: Creation &mdash; roughly the first three months</h2>
    <p>This phase runs like a focused project:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>A core project team</strong> of 8&ndash;12 people &mdash; the sweet spot for efficient, interdisciplinary collaboration, regardless of how large the underlying issue is.</li>
      <li><strong>Executive sponsorship, non-negotiably.</strong> For smaller organizations, the owner or CEO. For larger ones, a country head, division CEO, or regional CEO. Change doesn&rsquo;t sustain itself if it isn&rsquo;t visibly backed from the top.</li>
      <li><strong>Broader organizational input</strong>, typically gathered through targeted surveys &mdash; in past engagements we&rsquo;ve captured input from hundreds of employees to make sure the core team&rsquo;s thinking reflects the organization&rsquo;s actual reality.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>A team-efficiency tool runs throughout the entire process. We never work without one, and for 20 years it&rsquo;s been the same tool: <a href="/hbdi/">HBDI</a>, which we&rsquo;ve used with more than 3,500 professionals across 500+ teams. It gives every project team a shared, more precise language from day one.</p>
    <div class="table-scroll">
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Phase</th><th>Dominant focus</th><th>What&rsquo;s happening</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Early creation</td><td>Yellow (ideas) + Blue (analysis)</td><td>Understanding the real situation and generating possibilities</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Approaching implementation</td><td>Red (values, connection)</td><td>Bringing people along, building genuine buy-in</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Delivery</td><td>Green (order, process)</td><td>Disciplined execution</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <h2>Phase 2: Delivery &mdash; the Project Management Office</h2>
    <p>We define strategy as a magnetic field pulling toward a common point &mdash; the force that aligns every decision made across the organization, every day, toward the same outcome. The average professional makes roughly 20 decisions a day; a 30-person team makes around 600. Left to individual judgment, those 600 decisions scatter. A clearly defined, well-communicated strategy is what pulls them into alignment.</p>
    <ul>
      <li>A recurring one-hour weekly review of all active actions</li>
      <li>Standard project-management discipline, familiar to virtually every organization</li>
      <li>An external facilitator on the call each week, asking the questions that keep accountability alive</li>
    </ul>
    <p>It&rsquo;s precisely the &ldquo;sticky&rdquo; issues &mdash; the ones that matter but never quite become anyone&rsquo;s full-time priority &mdash; that get resolved once there&rsquo;s a person in the room every week whose job is to ask about them. Several clients have made this a standing part of how they operate, in relationships that have now run for over ten years.</p>
    <h2>What&rsquo;s included</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>A complete strategy deck, built collaboratively with your core team</li>
      <li>Weekly PMO facilitation through to the finish line</li>
      <li>A visibly more efficient, more aligned team by the end of the engagement</li>
      <li>An organizational learning process with benefits that extend well beyond the original issue</li>
    </ul>
  </div>
</section>

